 ### Backward-edge routing (#18)
 
 Loop-back connections (below) and self-loops always use the routed `orthogonal` edge type (named `backward` before #44, which draws forward edges with it too). Other connections compare the **measured output tip's x** with the **input tip's x**: an output at or to the right of the input routes backward. Once classified, an 8 px hysteresis band keeps small moves from switching the shape (a backward connection becomes forward below -8 px; a forward one becomes backward above +8 px). This includes stacked cards without a one-pixel card-position boundary. The band is not persisted: a drag that ends inside it can show the other shape after a reload, which classifies afresh. Unselected forward edges without a manual route retain xyflow's `smoothstep` type and path, including when a label moves (the orthogonal edge draws the same path while one is selected) and remain outside #18's obstacle-avoidance scope; an author can reroute one by hand (#44).
